(2417375, 330790, 28710, '<td valign="top">Ok, please don''t take this as a ''narrow minded comment'' because I am pretty open here. I used to write some small time fantasy fiction for a couple of sites on the net...<br/> <br/>I am going to say that I found the pic hard to vote on because the subject of the title did not match the subject of the photo.<br/> <br/>I looked at a lot of things in this pic, including the lighting, the composition, the makeup, the lines, the colors, and in the end, I couldn''t come up with a vote. I skipped it. I don''t do this very often at all. <br/> <br/>What I think might be the issue rather than the ''possessed'' quality of the carrot is the balance between your two subjects. I think you have tried to balance them a bit too much. The picture seems to be about as much about ''Diana'' as it is about the carrot...<br/> <br/>The title is worded in such a way that the carrot should take center stage and the model should be playing second fiddle. <br/> <br/>There are a few different ways that could have been accomplished. I would probably have gone for either DOF or Lighting to shift the balance ever so slightly towards the carrot.<br/> <br/>Perhaps pushing the saturation a bit on the carrot and darkening everything else a bit might keep the picture as is, but make it more suited to the title.<br/> <br/>Just some ideas...<br/> <br/></td>', 'Ok, please don''t take this as a ''narrow minded comment'' because I am pretty open here. I used to write some small time fantasy fiction for a couple of sites on the net... I am going to say that I found the pic hard to vote on because the subject of the title did not match the subject of the photo. I looked at a lot of things in this pic, including the lighting, the composition, the makeup, the lines, the colors, and in the end, I couldn''t come up with a vote. I skipped it. I don''t do this very often at all.  What I think might be the issue rather than the ''possessed'' quality of the carrot is the balance between your two subjects. I think you have tried to balance them a bit too much. The picture seems to be about as much about ''Diana'' as it is about the carrot... The title is worded in such a way that the carrot should take center stage and the model should be playing second fiddle.  There are a few different ways that could have been accomplished. I would probably have gone for either DOF or Lighting to shift the balance ever so slightly towards the carrot. Perhaps pushing the saturation a bit on the carrot and darkening everything else a bit might keep the picture as is, but make it more suited to the title. Just some ideas... ', '2006-05-15 12:48:55.000000', NULL, 0),
